lesson: 67
title: Skip counting by twos, fives and tens
stars: 0
explain: When every group has the same number, count by that number: by 2s for pairs, by 5s for hands, by 10s for tens.
explain: 4 pairs of socks are counted 2, 4, 6, 8, so there are 8 socks.
explain: 3 groups of 5 are counted 5, 10, 15. The last number said is the total.
vars: a=name t=thing k=2..10 n=2..10
need: k == 2 || k == 5 || k == 10
answer: n * k
exercise: {a} puts {k} {t} in each bag and fills {n} bags. Counting by {k}s, how many {t} are there?
exercise: {a} counts by {k}s and says {n} numbers, starting at {k}. What is the last number {a.he} says?
exercise: There are {n} rows of chairs with {k} chairs in each row. How many chairs are there?
exercise: Each plate has {k} cookies. There are {n} plates. How many cookies are there in all?
exercise: {a} jumps {k} squares at a time along a number track starting at 0. Where is {a.he} after {n} jumps?
test: Counting {n} groups of {k}, what total do you reach?
test: {n} children each bring {k} {t} to class. How many {t} come to class?
test: Skip counting by {k}, {a} goes {k}, {2*k}, {3*k}, and so on. What is the {n.ordwords} number {a.he} says?
exercise: {a} has {n} bags with {k} {t} in every bag. How many {t} does {a.he} have altogether?
exercise: Count by {k}s, {n} times, starting at 0. Where do you stop?
exercise: On a farm in Kent, {n} hens each lay {k} eggs in a week. How many eggs is that?
exercise: {a} puts {k} {t} on every plate and sets out {n} plates. How many {t} are on the plates in total?
exercise: Each pack of {t} in a Toronto shop holds {k}. {a} buys {n} packs. How many {t} does {a.he} buy?
exercise: {n} children in a Melbourne class each hold up {k} fingers. How many fingers are up in all?
exercise: {a} saves {k} dollars every week for {n} weeks. How many dollars does {a.he} save?
exercise: What is {n} groups of {k}?
test: A Dublin bus stops {n} times, and at every stop {k} people get on. Nobody gets off. How many people got on?
test: {n} lots of {k} make how many?
test: How many {t} do {n} boxes hold, if every box holds {k}?
test: {a} counts by {k}s on a hopscotch path in Vermont, one number per hop, starting with {k}. What number does {a.he} say on hop number {n}?
test: There are {k} chairs per table, and a hall in Wellington has {n} tables. How many chairs are there?
test: {k} {t} apiece for {n} friends: how many {t} is that in total?
exercise: {n} bags, {k} {t} each, count by {k}s. how many
exercise: {n} rows, {k} chairs a row. how many chairs
exercise: counting by {k}s, {n} numbers starting at {k}. last one you say?
exercise: Kai counts {n} bags of {k} by {k}s and gets {n*k+k}, he counted one bag twice. whats right
test: {a} has {n+1} — no sorry, {n} — packs of {k} {t}. how many {t}
test: {n} stacks of {k} coins, and theres a 20 note too. how many coins
test: skip count {k}s, {n} jumps from 0, where u land
exam: Determine the aggregate quantity of {t} contained in {n.words} bags, each of which contains {k.words} {t}.
exam: A pupil enumerates in multiples of {k}, commencing at {k}, and pronounces {n} numbers in total. State the final number pronounced.
exam: It is given that {n} receptacles are filled, each with {k} {t}. Ascertain the total number of {t} so packed.
exam: Compute the {n.ordwords} term of the sequence of multiples of {k} which commences {k}, {2*k}, {3*k}.
exercise: Grandma passed away and left {n} envelopes for the grandchildren, with {k} dollars in each. How many dollars did she leave in all?
exercise: Since Mum was made redundant, she sells eggs at the gate. Today she sold {n} boxes of {k}. Counting by {k}s, how many eggs did she sell?
test: crap, {a} 'misremembered' — said {a.he} packed {n} bags with {k+1} {t} each. it was {k} each. how many {t} then, count by {k}s
